STEP 3 -- interface cost changes the metric, and OSPF's AD of 110 loses to a static
          route in the dataplane                                                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Guide authority, C613-22055-00 REV E p.11, Table 2 (Administrative Distance):
  "Administrative Distance is a measure of the trustworthiness of the source of the
  routing information. The lower the AD value, the more reliable the routing protocol
  is considered." (Connected 0, Static 1, ... OSPF 110.)

  3a. DEFAULT INTERFACE COST, read before changing it (21:46:26):

awplus# show ip ospf interface vlan61
  vlan61 is up, line protocol is up
    Internet Address 10.0.61.1/30, Area 0.0.0.0, MTU 1500
    Interface state Backup
    Process ID 1, Router ID 1.1.1.1, Network Type BROADCAST, Cost: 1
    Designated Router (ID) 2.2.2.2, Interface Address 10.0.61.2
    Backup Designated Router (ID) 1.1.1.1, Interface Address 10.0.61.1
    Timer intervals configured, Hello 10, Dead 40, Wait 40, Retransmit 5
    Neighbor Count is 1, Adjacent neighbor count is 1
    Hello received 44 sent 45, DD received 3 sent 4
    LS-Req received 1 sent 1, LS-Upd received 5 sent 5

  Default cost on this 1 G SVI is 1, which is why the learned metric was [110/2]
  (1 transit + 1 stub). swi_b is the DR on the higher router-id 2.2.2.2, the DUT the BDR.

  3b. `ip ospf cost 50` on the DUT's vlan61 (21:47:07):

awplus(config)# interface vlan61
awplus(config-if)# ip ospf cost 50

    polled on the DUT:
      21:46:45  O 10.70.70.1/32 [110/51] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:00:10   <-- 50 + 1
                O 10.90.90.1/32 [110/2]  via 10.0.41.2, vlan41, 00:07:24   <-- untouched

  The cost applies to the interface the route is learned THROUGH, and only that route's
  metric moves. Reverted immediately with `no ip ospf cost` (21:47:08), metric back to 2.
  This is a read-back limb: each loopback has exactly one way in on this bench, so there
  is no path for the cost to move.

  3c. A COMPETING STATIC ROUTE, AD 1 vs OSPF's 110 (21:47:10):

awplus(config)# ip route 10.70.70.1/32 10.0.41.2    <-- points at swi_a, which does NOT
                                                        own the prefix. Deliberate: the
                                                        wire can then say which route was
                                                        used
awplus# show ip route database        (legend: > selected, * FIB)
  S    *> 10.70.70.1/32 [1/0] via 10.0.41.2, vlan41 weight 1     <-- the static takes the FIB
  O       10.70.70.1/32 [110/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:00:15  <-- OSPF demoted to RIB only
  O    *> 10.90.90.1/32 [110/2] via 10.0.41.2, vlan41, 00:08:04
awplus# show ip route 10.70.70.1
  Routing entry for 10.70.70.1/32
    Known via "static", distance 1, metric 0, best
    * via 10.0.41.2, vlan41 weight 1

  STP immediately before the leg (21:47:28): port1.0.4, port1.0.10, port1.0.16 all
  Designated Forwarding.

    --tag L4-static-beats-ospf.  cap L4-static-beats-ospf-eth3.pcap: 38 frame(s)
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 10.70.70.1   [ours]
      x5  ICMP t11/c0 10.0.41.2 -> 192.168.10.5   [ours]   <-- TIME EXCEEDED, SOURCED BY
                                                               swi_a
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 10.90.90.1   [ours]
      x5  ICMP t0/c0 10.90.90.1 -> 192.168.10.5   [ours]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 203.0.113.9  [ours]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 / x5 t0/c0 192.168.10.1      [ours]
      x2  ICMP t3/c0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5 [ours]   <-- 203.0.113.9 still dropped

      10.70.70.1   : 5 sent, 0 echo replies, and 5 ICMP TIME-EXCEEDED from 10.0.41.2
      10.90.90.1   : 5/5      203.0.113.9 : 0/5      192.168.10.1 : 5/5

  The wire names the winner. Those type-11s could only be produced by swi_a, so the DUT
  handed the frames to 10.0.41.2 -- the STATIC next hop -- and not to 10.0.61.2. swi_a
  in turn has its own OSPF route for 10.70.70.1/32 pointing back at the DUT, its only
  OSPF neighbour, so the two bounced the packet until TTL ran out and swi_a reported it.
  With the OSPF route in use there is no loop at all (STEPs 1 and 2e), so a time-exceeded
  is positive proof the static displaced OSPF in the DATAPLANE and not merely in the
  display. These were 5 for 5, not rate-limited, unlike the network-unreachables at 2
  per 15.

  3d. REMOVE THE STATIC -- OSPF RESUMES (21:48:3x):

awplus(config)# no ip route 10.70.70.1/32 10.0.41.2
awplus# show ip route database
  O    *> 10.70.70.1/32 [110/2] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, 00:01:24   <-- OSPF back in the FIB
awplus# show running-config | include ip route
awplus#                                                           <-- EMPTY again

    --tag L4b-ospf-resumes.  cap 38 frame(s)
      10.70.70.1 : 5 sent, 5 echo replies sourced by 10.70.70.1 -- and ZERO time-exceeded
      10.90.90.1 : 5/5      203.0.113.9 : 0/5      192.168.10.1 : 5/5

  => STEP 3 PASS: the default interface cost of 1 is read back from the DUT and explains
     the learned metric of 2; `ip ospf cost 50` moves that one route to [110/51] and
     nothing else. OSPF's administrative distance of 110 behaves exactly as the guide
     documents, and the preference was measured where it counts -- the egress the frames
     actually took, attributed by the next hop's own ICMP -- then reversed, with the
     echo replies and the absence of time-exceeded proving OSPF took the dataplane back.

